%I A400826 #7 Oct 19 2026 17:59:27
%S A400826 0,1,1,0,2,0,2,1,2,0,1,1,2,0,1,0,2,0,1,1,2,0,2,0,2,0,1,1,2,0,2,1,2,0,
%T A400826 1,1,2,0,2,0,2,0,1,1,2,0,1,1,2,0,1,1,2,0,2,0,2,0,1,1,2,0,1,0,2,0,1,1,
%U A400826 2,0,2,0,2,0,1,1,2,0,1,1,2,0,1,1,2,0,2
%N A400826 Grundy value of n in the impartial game in which from even n the only move is to n/2, from odd n > 1 the moves are to 3n+1 or to (3n+1)/2, and 1 has no moves.
%C A400826 The moves are those of the Collatz map (A006370) together with its shortcut (3n+1)/2 (A014682) for odd n; the game is played under the normal play convention (the player who cannot move loses).
%C A400826 The formula in the FORMULA section defines a(n) for every n >= 1 without any assumption. It gives the Grundy value of n whenever the Collatz trajectory of n reaches 1, so that the game starting at n is finite; this is known to hold for all n < 2^68 (Barina, 2021).
%C A400826 By the Sprague-Grundy theorem, the Grundy value of a position is the mex of the values of its options, and the second player wins from n if and only if a(n) = 0.
%C A400826 All positions reachable from n lie on the Collatz trajectory of n, so the game starting at n is finite if and only if the trajectory of n reaches 1.
%C A400826 For odd n > 1, a(n) = 2 if the binary expansion of n ends with an odd number of 1's, and a(n) = 1 otherwise. For even n not a power of 2, a(n) = 0 if the binary expansion of n ends with an odd number of 0's, and a(n) = 1 otherwise.
%C A400826 Thus a(n) depends only on the trailing binary digits of n, while the finiteness of the game depends on the whole trajectory.
%C A400826 Each of the values 0, 1, 2 occurs with asymptotic density 1/3.
%D A400826 David Barina, Convergence verification of the Collatz problem, J. Supercomputing 77 (2021), 2681-2688.
%H A400826 Miguel Cerdá Bennassar, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23207880">El juego de Collatz en el sentido de Conway. Valores de Grundy locales y terminación global</a>, Zenodo, 2026 (in Spanish).
%F A400826 a(1) = 0.
%F A400826 For odd n > 1: a(n) = 1 + A096268(n).
%F A400826 For even n not a power of 2: a(n) = 1 - A096268(n-1).
%F A400826 a(2^v) = A096268(2^v - 1) = v mod 2.
%e A400826 a(5) = 2: the options of 5 are 16 and 8, with a(16) = 0 and a(8) = 1, so a(5) = mex{0, 1} = 2.
%e A400826 a(3) = 1: the options of 3 are 10 and 5, with a(10) = 0 and a(5) = 2, so a(3) = mex{0, 2} = 1.
%e A400826 In binary: 7 = 111_2 ends with three 1's, so a(7) = 2; 15 = 1111_2 ends with four 1's, so a(15) = 1; 12 = 1100_2 ends with two 0's, so a(12) = 1.
%t A400826 a[n_] := Which[n == 1, 0, OddQ[n], 1 + Mod[IntegerExponent[n + 1, 2], 2], IntegerQ[Log2[n]], Mod[IntegerExponent[n, 2], 2], True, 1 - Mod[IntegerExponent[n, 2], 2]]; Array[a, 87]
%o A400826 (PARI) a(n) = if(n==1, 0, n%2, if(valuation(n+1, 2)%2, 2, 1), my(v=valuation(n, 2)); if(n>>v==1, v%2, 1-v%2))
%o A400826 (Python)
%o A400826 def a(n):
%o A400826     if n == 1: return 0
%o A400826     if n & (n-1) == 0: return (n.bit_length()-1) % 2
%o A400826     if n % 2 == 0:
%o A400826         v = (n & -n).bit_length() - 1
%o A400826         return 1 - v % 2
%o A400826     v = ((n+1) & -(n+1)).bit_length() - 1
%o A400826     return 1 + v % 2
%Y A400826 Cf. A096268, A006370, A014682, A007814, A006577.
%K A400826 nonn,easy,new
%O A400826 1,5
%A A400826 _Miquel Cerda_, Oct 07 2026
